@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Israel: the rules for counting time, as executable functions.
--
-- Sources (unofficial Wikisource consolidations; the official texts could not be fetched):
--   the Interpretation Law 5741-1981, ../../source/law.wiki, cited as src:N;
--   the Interpretation Ordinance [New Version], ../../source/ordinance.wiki, cited as src:ordinance:N.
-- This module imports only `prelude` and `daydate`, so another encoding can import it alone.
--
-- Days of rest, recess and holiday are an INPUT: every function that needs them takes a function
-- from DATE to BOOLEAN (or a list, through `days of rest, recess or holiday listed in`).
-- No holiday is hard-coded and the Hebrew calendar is not computed.
--
-- Whether Law s 1's gate lets the Law govern a given matter ("if there is no other provision on
-- the matter and nothing in the matter or its context is inconsistent") is the CALLER's question;
-- see `s 1 — the Law governs` in il-interpretation-law.l4.
--
-- Dates are counted as whole days, in Gregorian terms (Law s 3), and moments are civil time.

§ `Counting time: Interpretation Law ss 3, 8, 10, 21 and Interpretation Ordinance s 17`

§§ `Nouns`

-- A moment of civil time: a day and a time of day on it.
-- Midnight at the END of a day is the first moment (00:00:00) of the next day.
-- `daydate` has no time of day and this module may import nothing else, so the time is three numbers.
DECLARE Moment HAS
    `the day`  IS A DATE
    `hour`     IS A NUMBER
    `minute`   IS A NUMBER
    `second`   IS A NUMBER

-- The two calendars Law s 3 distinguishes for "year" and "month".
DECLARE Calendar IS ONE OF
    `the Gregorian calendar`
    `the Hebrew calendar`

-- How the beginning and the end of a period of years or months were specified in the enactment (Law s 3).
DECLARE `Specification of a period of years or months` HAS
    `beginning by the Hebrew calendar only`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `end by the Hebrew calendar only`        IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `Named refusals`

-- Each refusal is returned, never stored (an unforced REFUSE is no refusal); the name is the message.

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`a period counted by the Hebrew calendar needs the Hebrew calendar's months, their lengths and its leap years, which this module does not compute` MEANS
    REFUSE "a period counted by the Hebrew calendar needs the Hebrew calendar's months, their lengths and its leap years, which this module does not compute"

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`s 10 speaks of a period fixed in a number of days, weeks, months or years; the number given is not a positive whole number` MEANS
    REFUSE "s 10 speaks of a period fixed in a number of days, weeks, months or years; the number given is not a positive whole number"

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`s 10(c) gives no answer if the supplied calendar of rest days has no day that is not a rest day within a year` MEANS
    REFUSE "s 10(c) gives no answer if the supplied calendar of rest days has no day that is not a rest day within a year"

§§ `Moments`

GIVEN d IS A DATE
      h IS A NUMBER
      m IS A NUMBER
      s IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A Moment
`the moment on` d `at` h `:` m `:` s MEANS
    Moment WITH `the day` IS d, `hour` IS h, `minute` IS m, `second` IS s

-- The number of seconds from the first day `daydate` counts to the moment: a single scale for comparing moments.
GIVEN m IS A Moment
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of seconds up to` m MEANS
    (Day (m's `the day`) TIMES 86400) PLUS (m's `hour` TIMES 3600) PLUS (m's `minute` TIMES 60) PLUS m's `second`

§§ `s 8 — "until" includes the day it names`

-- src:77 | הביטוי ”עד...“ לענין זמן או לענין מובאה – משמעו עד ועד בכלל.
--
-- Paraphrase (mine; the Hebrew governs): the expression "until ..." in relation to time, or in relation to a
-- quotation, means until and including.
-- This settles a case a convention would otherwise have to settle: a date bound written "until D" includes D.
-- The text is silent about the other end ("from D"); that is s 10(a)'s business for periods and is not
-- settled here for dates in general.
@ref Interpretation Law s 8 (src:77)
GIVEN x IS A DATE
      limit IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 8 — the date` x `is up to and including` limit MEANS
    x AT MOST limit

-- "לענין מובאה": in relation to a quotation or citation, e.g. "sections 3 until 5" includes section 5.
@ref Interpretation Law s 8 (src:77)
GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      limit IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 8 — the number` x `is up to and including` limit MEANS
    x AT MOST limit

@ref Interpretation Law s 8 (src:77)
GIVEN m IS A Moment
      limit IS A Moment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 8 — the moment` m `is up to and including` limit MEANS
    `the number of seconds up to` m AT MOST `the number of seconds up to` limit

§§ `s 3 — "day", "year", "month", "fiscal year"`

-- src:43 | ”יום“ – תקופה מחצות הלילה עד חצות הלילה שלאחריו;
--
-- Paraphrase (mine): "day" is the period from midnight until the midnight that follows it.
-- READING TAKEN, see the fork register in NOTES.md: s 3's own "עד" is read with s 8, so the closing midnight
-- belongs to the day as well as to the next one. `the day of the moment` still gives a moment its own day
-- (the moment 00:00:00 is on the day it begins). The half-open alternative is offered beside it.
@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "day" (src:43)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A Moment
`s 3 — the midnight that begins the day` d MEANS
    `the moment on` d `at` 0 `:` 0 `:` 0

@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "day" (src:43)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A Moment
`s 3 — the midnight that ends the day` d MEANS
    `the moment on` (d PLUS 1) `at` 0 `:` 0 `:` 0

@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "day" (src:43)
GIVEN m IS A Moment
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 3 — the moment` m `is within the day` d MEANS
        `s 8 — the moment` (`s 3 — the midnight that begins the day` d) `is up to and including` m
    AND `s 8 — the moment` m `is up to and including` (`s 3 — the midnight that ends the day` d)

@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "day" (src:43)
GIVEN m IS A Moment
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 3 — the moment` m `is within the day` d `, not counting the closing midnight` MEANS
        `s 8 — the moment` (`s 3 — the midnight that begins the day` d) `is up to and including` m
    AND `the number of seconds up to` m LESS THAN `the number of seconds up to` (`s 3 — the midnight that ends the day` d)

@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "day" (src:43)
GIVEN m IS A Moment
GIVETH A DATE
`s 3 — the day of the moment` m MEANS
    m's `the day`

-- src:57 | ”שנה“ ו”חודש“ – לפי הלוח הגריגוריאני, ואם צויינה תחילת תקופתם או סופה לפי הלוח העברי בלבד – לפי הלוח העברי;
--
-- Paraphrase (mine): "year" and "month" are by the Gregorian calendar, and if the beginning of their period or
-- its end was specified by the Hebrew calendar only, by the Hebrew calendar.
@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "year" and "month" (src:57)
GIVEN spec IS A `Specification of a period of years or months`
GIVETH A Calendar
`s 3 — the calendar of a year or month specified as` spec MEANS
    IF   spec's `beginning by the Hebrew calendar only` OR spec's `end by the Hebrew calendar only`
    THEN `the Hebrew calendar`
    ELSE `the Gregorian calendar`

-- src:58 | ”שנת כספים“ – תקופה מאחד בינואר של שנה פלונית עד שלושים ואחד בדצמבר של אותה שנה; על אף האמור
-- src:58 | בסעיף 1 תחול הגדרה זו גם על חיקוקים והוראות מינהל שניתנו לפני תחילתו של חוק זה;
--
-- Paraphrase (mine): "fiscal year" is the period from the first of January of a given year until the thirty-first
-- of December of that year; notwithstanding s 1 this definition applies also to enactments and administrative
-- directions given before the Law came into force.
@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "fiscal year" (src:58)
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`s 3 — the first day of the fiscal year` y MEANS
    YMD y 1 1

@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "fiscal year" (src:58)
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`s 3 — the last day of the fiscal year` y MEANS
    YMD y 12 31

@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "fiscal year" (src:58); s 8 (src:77)
GIVEN x IS A DATE
      y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 3 — the date` x `is in the fiscal year` y MEANS
        x AT LEAST `s 3 — the first day of the fiscal year` y
    AND `s 8 — the date` x `is up to and including` (`s 3 — the last day of the fiscal year` y)

§§ `s 10 — periods`

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10 — the number is a positive whole number of days, weeks, months or years` n MEANS
        n AT LEAST 1
    AND n EQUALS (TRUNC n 0)

-- src:83 | (א) מקום שנקבעה תקופה קצובה במספר ימים או שבועות מיום פלוני, אותו יום לא יבוא במנין.
-- src:84 | (ב) תקופה קצובה במספר חדשים או שנים לאחר אירוע פלוני תסתיים בחדשה האחרון ביום שמספרו בחודש כמספר יום האירוע, ואם היה החודש חסר אותו יום – ביום האחרון של החודש.
-- src:85 | (ג) במנין ימי תקופה יבואו גם ימי מנוחה, פגרה או שבתון שעל פי חיקוק, זולת אם הם הימים האחרונים שבתקופה.
--
-- Paraphrase (mine; the Hebrew governs):
-- (a) where a fixed period in a number of days or weeks is set from a certain day, that day is not counted;
-- (b) a fixed period in a number of months or years after a certain event ends in the last month on the day
--     whose number in the month is the number of the day of the event, and if the month lacks that day, on the
--     last day of the month;
-- (c) in counting the days of a period, days of rest, recess or holiday under an enactment are counted too,
--     unless they are the last days of the period.
-- (Line 84 reads "בחדשה האחרון" where "בחדש האחרון", "in the last month", is evidently meant; the stray ה is
-- in the Wikisource text. It does not change the meaning.)
--
-- The unit decides which of (a) and (b) governs, because that is what each subsection's words restrict:
-- (a) days and weeks, (b) months and years. A period of months or years written "from" a day is therefore
-- (b), with the day as the day of the event; (a) cannot govern it, its words stop at days and weeks.

-- (a): the day itself is not counted, so the counted days are D+1 .. D+N and the last day is D+N.
-- A reader expecting D+N-1 is counting D; (a) says D "לא יבוא במנין" (shall not come into the count).
@ref Interpretation Law s 10(a) (src:83-85)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(a) — the last day of a period of` n `days from` d MEANS
    IF   `s 10 — the number is a positive whole number of days, weeks, months or years` n
    THEN d PLUS n
    ELSE `s 10 speaks of a period fixed in a number of days, weeks, months or years; the number given is not a positive whole number`

-- A week is taken as seven days: the Law defines no "week" (a fork, see NOTES.md).
@ref Interpretation Law s 10(a) (src:83)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(a) — the last day of a period of` n `weeks from` d MEANS
    IF   `s 10 — the number is a positive whole number of days, weeks, months or years` n
    THEN d PLUS (n TIMES 7)
    ELSE `s 10 speaks of a period fixed in a number of days, weeks, months or years; the number given is not a positive whole number`

-- "After an event", for days and weeks: (a) governs, the day of the event being the day the period is "from".
-- (b)'s "לאחר אירוע" is confined by its unit to months and years, as (a)'s "מיום" is to days and weeks.
@ref Interpretation Law s 10(a) (src:83)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10 — the last day of a period of` n `days after the day` d MEANS
    `s 10(a) — the last day of a period of` n `days from` d

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(a) (src:83)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10 — the last day of a period of` n `weeks after the day` d MEANS
    `s 10(a) — the last day of a period of` n `weeks from` d

-- (b), written out from the statute's words and not by calling `add months`: the month of the end is the month
-- of the event plus N months (12N for years), the day is the event's day number, and if that month lacks
-- the day, it is the last day of that month. `daydate`'s `add months` was measured to agree on starts on the
-- 31st and on 29 February, and the tests assert that agreement, but the statute governs.
@ref Interpretation Law s 10(b) (src:84)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `months after an event on` d MEANS
    IF   `s 10 — the number is a positive whole number of days, weeks, months or years` n
    THEN YMD `year of the end` `month of the end` `day of the end`
    ELSE `s 10 speaks of a period fixed in a number of days, weeks, months or years; the number given is not a positive whole number`
    WHERE
        `months from the year 0` MEANS (DATE_YEAR d TIMES 12) PLUS (DATE_MONTH d MINUS 1) PLUS n
        `month of the end`       MEANS (`months from the year 0` MODULO 12) PLUS 1
        `year of the end`        MEANS (`months from the year 0` MINUS (`months from the year 0` MODULO 12)) DIVIDED BY 12
        `days in the month of the end` MEANS `Days in month` `month of the end` `year of the end`
        `day of the end`         MEANS
            IF   DATE_DAY d AT MOST `days in the month of the end`
            THEN DATE_DAY d
            ELSE `days in the month of the end`

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(b) (src:84)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `years after an event on` d MEANS
    IF   `s 10 — the number is a positive whole number of days, weeks, months or years` n
    THEN `s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` (n TIMES 12) `months after an event on` d
    ELSE `s 10 speaks of a period fixed in a number of days, weeks, months or years; the number given is not a positive whole number`

-- "From" a day, for months and years: (b) governs, the day being the day of the event (see above).
@ref Interpretation Law s 10(b) (src:84)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10 — the last day of a period of` n `months from the day` d MEANS
    `s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `months after an event on` d

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(b) (src:84)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10 — the last day of a period of` n `years from the day` d MEANS
    `s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `years after an event on` d

-- Law s 3 makes "year" and "month" Hebrew-calendar units when the enactment specified the beginning or the
-- end of the period by the Hebrew calendar only. This module does not compute the Hebrew calendar.
@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "year" and "month" (src:57); s 10(b) (src:84)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      cal IS A Calendar
GIVETH A DATE
`s 3 and s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `months after an event on` d `, counted by` cal MEANS
    CONSIDER cal
    WHEN `the Gregorian calendar` THEN `s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `months after an event on` d
    WHEN `the Hebrew calendar`    THEN `a period counted by the Hebrew calendar needs the Hebrew calendar's months, their lengths and its leap years, which this module does not compute`

@ref Interpretation Law s 3 "year" and "month" (src:57); s 10(b) (src:84)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      cal IS A Calendar
GIVETH A DATE
`s 3 and s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `years after an event on` d `, counted by` cal MEANS
    CONSIDER cal
    WHEN `the Gregorian calendar` THEN `s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `years after an event on` d
    WHEN `the Hebrew calendar`    THEN `a period counted by the Hebrew calendar needs the Hebrew calendar's months, their lengths and its leap years, which this module does not compute`

-- The first day of the period. (a) says the day of the start is not counted, so counting begins the next day.
-- (b) is silent about the start, but its end rule fixes it: a month "after" an event on 15 January ends on
-- 15 February, so the period is 16 January to 15 February and the event's own day is outside it.
@ref Interpretation Law s 10(a) (src:83); s 10(b) (src:84)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10 — the first day of a period counted from or after an event on` d MEANS
    d PLUS 1

-- (c): the period does not end on a day of rest, recess or holiday. It ends on the first day, from the nominal
-- last day onwards, that is not one. Days of rest, recess or holiday earlier in the period are counted as
-- days of the period, so they never move the end. See NOTES.md for the readings considered.
-- The walk is bounded by 366 days. The statute sets no bound; the bound is this encoding's, so that a
-- calendar that names every day a rest day gives a named refusal and not a loop.
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
      fuel IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day from` d `on that is not a day of rest, recess or holiday under` rest `, within` fuel `more days` MEANS
    IF   NOT rest d
    THEN d
    ELSE IF fuel AT MOST 0
         THEN `s 10(c) gives no answer if the supplied calendar of rest days has no day that is not a rest day within a year`
         ELSE `the first day from` (d PLUS 1) `on that is not a day of rest, recess or holiday under` rest `, within` (fuel MINUS 1) `more days`

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(c) (src:85)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(c) — the last day of a period whose nominal last day is` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest MEANS
    `the first day from` d `on that is not a day of rest, recess or holiday under` rest `, within` 366 `more days`

-- The same rule for a calendar of rest days given as a list of dates.
GIVEN days IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
`days of rest, recess or holiday listed in` days MEANS
    GIVEN day YIELD elem day days

-- The composed forms. s 10(c) is applied to every unit, months and years included: its words speak of "ימי
-- תקופה" (the days of a period), not of periods in days only (a fork, see NOTES.md). A caller who reads (c) as
-- limited to days and weeks uses the s 10(a)/(b) functions above alone for months and years.
@ref Interpretation Law s 10(a) (src:83); s 10(c) (src:85)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(a), (c) — the last day of a period of` n `days from` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest MEANS
    `s 10(c) — the last day of a period whose nominal last day is` (`s 10(a) — the last day of a period of` n `days from` d) `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(a) (src:83); s 10(c) (src:85)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(a), (c) — the last day of a period of` n `weeks from` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest MEANS
    `s 10(c) — the last day of a period whose nominal last day is` (`s 10(a) — the last day of a period of` n `weeks from` d) `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(b) (src:84); s 10(c) (src:85)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(b), (c) — the last day of a period of` n `months after an event on` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest MEANS
    `s 10(c) — the last day of a period whose nominal last day is` (`s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `months after an event on` d) `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(b) (src:84); s 10(c) (src:85)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(b), (c) — the last day of a period of` n `years after an event on` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest MEANS
    `s 10(c) — the last day of a period whose nominal last day is` (`s 10(b) — the last day of a period of` n `years after an event on` d) `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest

-- Whether a date falls inside a period. The period runs from its first day to its last day, both inside:
-- the last day is the day on which the period "ends" (s 10(b)) or the Nth counted day (s 10(a)), and an
-- "until" bound includes the day it names (s 8).
@ref Interpretation Law s 8 (src:77); s 10(a) (src:83); s 10(b) (src:84)
GIVEN x IS A DATE
      first IS A DATE
      last IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 8, s 10 — the date` x `is inside the period beginning on` first `and ending on` last MEANS
        x AT LEAST first
    AND `s 8 — the date` x `is up to and including` last

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(a) (src:83); s 10(c) (src:85)
GIVEN x IS A DATE
      n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10 — the date` x `is inside a period of` n `days from` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest MEANS
    `s 8, s 10 — the date` x `is inside the period beginning on` (`s 10 — the first day of a period counted from or after an event on` d)
        `and ending on` (`s 10(a), (c) — the last day of a period of` n `days from` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest)

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(a) (src:83); s 10(c) (src:85)
GIVEN x IS A DATE
      n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10 — the date` x `is inside a period of` n `weeks from` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest MEANS
    `s 8, s 10 — the date` x `is inside the period beginning on` (`s 10 — the first day of a period counted from or after an event on` d)
        `and ending on` (`s 10(a), (c) — the last day of a period of` n `weeks from` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest)

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(b) (src:84); s 10(c) (src:85)
GIVEN x IS A DATE
      n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10 — the date` x `is inside a period of` n `months after an event on` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest MEANS
    `s 8, s 10 — the date` x `is inside the period beginning on` (`s 10 — the first day of a period counted from or after an event on` d)
        `and ending on` (`s 10(b), (c) — the last day of a period of` n `months after an event on` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest)

@ref Interpretation Law s 10(b) (src:84); s 10(c) (src:85)
GIVEN x IS A DATE
      n IS A NUMBER
      d IS A DATE
      rest IS A FUNCTION FROM DATE TO BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10 — the date` x `is inside a period of` n `years after an event on` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest MEANS
    `s 8, s 10 — the date` x `is inside the period beginning on` (`s 10 — the first day of a period counted from or after an event on` d)
        `and ending on` (`s 10(b), (c) — the last day of a period of` n `years after an event on` d `, when days of rest, recess or holiday are` rest)

§§ `s 21 — the moment a statute begins to bind`

-- src:124 | שעת תחילת תקפו של חיקוק היא בשעה 00.01 של יום תחילתו.
--
-- Paraphrase (mine): the time of the beginning of an enactment's validity is at 00.01 on the day of its commencement.
-- "00.01" is read as one minute past midnight, 00:01:00. At 00:00:00 to 00:00:59 on the commencement day the
-- enactment does not yet bind, so there is a one-minute gap at the start of that day.
@ref Interpretation Law s 21 (src:124)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A Moment
`s 21 — the moment a statute begins to bind, commencing on` d MEANS
    `the moment on` d `at` 0 `:` 1 `:` 0

@ref Interpretation Law s 21 (src:124)
GIVEN m IS A Moment
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 21 — the moment` m `is one at which a statute commencing on` d `binds` MEANS
    `s 8 — the moment` (`s 21 — the moment a statute begins to bind, commencing on` d) `is up to and including` m

-- If the statute fixes a moment of its own, the Law's default does not apply (Law s 1: "if there is no other
-- provision on the matter"). The statute's own moment is a MAYBE: NOTHING means it fixed none.
@ref Interpretation Law s 21 (src:124)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      own IS A MAYBE Moment
GIVETH A Moment
`s 21 — the moment a statute commencing on` d `begins to bind, unless it fixes a moment of its own:` own MEANS
    fromMaybe (`s 21 — the moment a statute begins to bind, commencing on` d) own

§§ `Interpretation Ordinance s 17 — regulations of legislative effect`

-- src:ordinance:173 | תקנות בנות־פעל תחיקתי יפורסמו ברשומות, ותחילת תקפן ביום פרסומן, אם אין הוראה אחרת בענין זה.
--
-- Paraphrase (mine): regulations of legislative effect shall be published in Reshumot, and their validity begins
-- on the day of their publication, if there is no other provision on the matter.
-- The day of publication is an input. A regulation not yet published has no day under this section.
-- "Another provision" is a MAYBE DATE: JUST the day that provision fixes, NOTHING if there is none. Where another
-- provision exists but fixes no day (for example, "on approval by a committee"), the caller supplies the day
-- once it is known; this function cannot say anything before then.
@ref Interpretation Ordinance s 17 (src:ordinance:173)
GIVEN p IS A DATE
      other IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`Ordinance s 17 — the day regulations of legislative effect come into force, published on` p `unless another provision fixes the day:` other MEANS
    fromMaybe p other

-- Read with Law s 21, the regulations bind from 00:01 on that day, which may be before the moment they were
-- actually published on it. The text does not say otherwise; see the open questions in NOTES.md.
@ref Interpretation Ordinance s 17 (src:ordinance:173); Interpretation Law s 21 (src:124)
GIVEN p IS A DATE
      other IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A Moment
`Ordinance s 17, Law s 21 — the moment regulations of legislative effect begin to bind, published on` p `unless another provision fixes the day:` other MEANS
    `s 21 — the moment a statute begins to bind, commencing on` (`Ordinance s 17 — the day regulations of legislative effect come into force, published on` p `unless another provision fixes the day:` other)
